# 2️⃣ Customer Segmentation (Basic RFM in Excel / Pandas) – Label Customers A / B / C

# 🎯 Objective
# The objective of this project is to divide customers into groups based on their buying behavior using RFM analysis (Recency, Frequency, Monetary).
# By using only coding, the project labels customers as A, B, or C so businesses can easily identify best customers, average customers, and low-value customers.

# 📌 Problem Statement
# Businesses collect transaction-level data such as CustomerID, PurchaseDate, TransactionAmount, OrderID, ProductInformation, and Location,
# but this data is often stored in raw form and not used effectively to understand customer value.
#
# Without analyzing this transaction data, businesses cannot clearly identify which customers purchase frequently, which customers spend more,
# and which customers have become inactive over time. This results in poor marketing decisions, weak customer retention strategies,
# and inefficient use of business resources.
#
# In this project, we use a real-world transaction dataset to build a fully programmatic customer segmentation system using RFM analysis.
# Using Python and Pandas, the transaction data is processed to calculate:
# - Recency: the number of days since a customer's most recent purchase using PurchaseDate
# - Frequency: the total number of orders placed by a customer using OrderID
# - Monetary: the total amount spent by a customer using TransactionAmount
#
# Based on these calculated RFM values, customers are automatically segmented and labeled as:
# - A – High-value and loyal customers who purchase recently, frequently, and spend more
# - B – Medium-value customers with moderate purchase behavior
# - C – Low-value or inactive customers with low engagement
#
# The entire segmentation process is done using code only, ensuring the solution is reproducible, scalable,
# and suitable for real-world business datasets, without any manual Excel-based calculations.

